From: Richard T. Carback III <rick.carback@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] rtc: qcom-glink: Succeed when SET_REAL_TIME succeeds

qcom_rtc_glink_set_time() sends SET_REAL_TIME, then always sets the time
property and derives a tick offset from GET_RTC_TICKS. On the ASUS Ascent
QN10 the firmware accepts SET_REAL_TIME and answers GET_REAL_TIME, but has
no tick source, so every set returned -EIO after the clock was already set
and the kernel's NTP RTC sync retried once per second ("no tick source for
offset").

Return success once SET_REAL_TIME succeeds; reads use GET_REAL_TIME first,
so the offset is only needed for firmware without it.

Local test patch for the QN10 7.4-rc1 kernel build.
---
diff --git a/drivers/rtc/rtc-qcom-glink.c b/drivers/rtc/rtc-qcom-glink.c
index 611f06a3c..61baf5f4f 100644
--- a/drivers/rtc/rtc-qcom-glink.c
+++ b/drivers/rtc/rtc-qcom-glink.c
@@ -289,9 +289,16 @@ static int qcom_rtc_glink_set_time(struct qcom_rtc_glink *rtc_glink,
 	if (!rtc_glink->allow_set_time)
 		return -EOPNOTSUPP;
 
+	/*
+	 * Firmware that implements SET_REAL_TIME keeps the wall clock itself
+	 * and answers GET_REAL_TIME, so the tick offset below is not needed.
+	 * Some firmware (ASUS Ascent QN10) has no tick source at all, and
+	 * failing here after a successful set made the RTC unusable.
+	 */
 	ret = qcom_rtc_glink_set_real_time(rtc_glink, time);
-	if (ret)
-		dev_warn(rtc_glink->dev, "0x69 failed (%d)\n", ret);
+	if (!ret)
+		return 0;
+	dev_warn(rtc_glink->dev, "0x69 failed (%d)\n", ret);
 
 	ret = qcom_rtc_glink_set_property(rtc_glink, QCOM_RTC_GLINK_TIME, (u32)t);
 	if (ret)
